\title{A Dimension-Free Isotropy Model for Bourgain's Slicing Problem:\\ The Discrete Cube}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{June 26, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Bourgain's slicing problem (the hyperplane conjecture) asks whether there exists a
universal constant $c>0$, independent of dimension, such that every convex body
$K\subseteq\R^n$ of volume $1$ admits a hyperplane section of $(n-1)$-dimensional
volume at least $c$. The problem is equivalent to a uniform upper bound on the
isotropic constant $L_K$, and its decisive structural notion is \emph{isotropic
position}: a body whose covariance matrix is a scalar multiple of the identity. We
isolate and rigorously verify the load-bearing structural phenomenon---identity
covariance forcing dimension-free isotropy---on a fully discrete model that
requires no measure theory: the uniform probability measure on the discrete cube
$\cube$. We prove that this measure is centered, that its covariance kernel
$T(k,l)=\sum_x x_k x_l$ equals $2^n\,\delta_{kl}$ in every dimension, and
consequently that every unit linear functional has variance exactly $1$,
independently of $n$. The entire argument reduces to a single sign-flip involution
on the index set, which simultaneously annihilates the first moment (centering)
and the off-diagonal second moments (de-correlation). The resulting identity
$\E[\inner{\theta}{x}^2]=\norm{\theta}^2$ is a Pythagorean/Parseval identity for an
orthonormal coordinate system, exhibiting the discrete cube as an exactly
isotropic body with isotropic constant $1$, uniformly in the dimension.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The slicing problem}
Let $K\subseteq\R^n$ be a convex body of Lebesgue volume $1$. A \emph{hyperplane
section} of $K$ is its intersection with an affine hyperplane
$H=\{x:\inner{\theta}{x}=t\}$, a set of dimension $n-1$. Bourgain's slicing problem
asks:

\begin{quote}
\textbf{(Slicing conjecture).} There is a universal constant $c>0$ such that for
every $n$ and every convex body $K\subseteq\R^n$ with $\vol_n(K)=1$, there exists a
hyperplane $H$ with $\vol_{n-1}(K\cap H)\geq c$.
\end{quote}

The qualifier ``universal''---one constant for all dimensions at once---is the
source of all difficulty. In any fixed dimension the existence of a large section
is elementary; the challenge is dimension-freeness. The problem was raised by
Bourgain in the 1980s and became a central question of asymptotic convex geometry;
it is now known to hold, after a long sequence of improvements culminating in
recent work establishing a bounded isotropic constant.

\subsection{Isotropic position and the isotropic constant}
The slicing conjecture is equivalent to a statement about \emph{isotropic
position}. Center $K$ at its barycenter and let $x$ be uniformly distributed on
$K$. The covariance matrix of $x$ is $\Sigma_K=\E[xx^\top]$. After an affine
volume-preserving map one may assume $\Sigma_K=L_K^2 I_n$ for a scalar $L_K>0$, the
\emph{isotropic constant}. In this position every unit functional has the same
variance, $\E[\inner{\theta}{x}^2]=L_K^2$. The slicing conjecture is equivalent to
$\sup_n\sup_K L_K<\infty$. The crux is therefore: identity-shaped covariance, with
a scalar that does not blow up as $n\to\infty$.

\subsection{Contribution}
Genuine $(n-1)$-volumes of hyperplane sections of convex bodies lie beyond current
formalized mathematics (the P\'olya / Hensley--Vaaler Fourier-analytic
section-volume formula is unavailable). We instead formalize the \emph{structural}
content of isotropic position on a discrete model where every quantity is a finite
sum: the uniform probability measure on the discrete cube $\cube$. We prove, with
no measure theory, that the measure is centered (Theorem~\ref{thm:centered}), that
its covariance kernel is the identity scaled by $2^n$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:covariance}), that every linear functional is centered and its
second moment equals the squared Euclidean norm of its coefficient vector
(Theorem~\ref{thm:isotropy}), and hence that every unit functional has variance
exactly $1$, dimension-free (Theorem~\ref{thm:dimfree}). The proof method is a
single sign-flip involution that, applied to two different summands, yields both
centering and de-correlation. The final identity is Pythagorean: variance equals
sum of squares.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $n\in\N$ and a point of the discrete cube is a bit-string
$x:\{1,\dots,n\}\to\{\mathrm{true},\mathrm{false}\}$.

\begin{definition}[Sign value]
For a bit $b$, $\sgn(b)=1$ if $b=\mathrm{true}$ and $\sgn(b)=-1$ if
$b=\mathrm{false}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Coordinate]
For a cube point $x$ and index $i$, the $i$-th coordinate value is
$\coord(x,i)=\sgn(x_i)\in\{-1,+1\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Uniform expectation]
For $f:\cube\to\R$, $\displaystyle \E[f]=\frac{1}{2^n}\sum_{x\in\cube} f(x)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Coordinate flip]
For an index $i$, the map $\flip_i(x)$ toggles the $i$-th bit and fixes the rest:
$\flip_i(x)=\mathrm{update}(x,i,\lnot x_i)$. Being an involution it induces a
permutation of the $2^n$ cube points.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Covariance kernel]
For indices $k,l$, $\displaystyle T(k,l)=\sum_{x\in\cube}\coord(x,k)\,\coord(x,l)$.
Dividing by $2^n$ gives the entries of the covariance matrix $\Sigma$ of a
uniformly random cube point.
\end{definition}

\section{Elementary lemmas}

\begin{lemma}[Sign negation]\label{lem:sgnnot}
$\sgn(\lnot b)=-\sgn(b)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof} Case analysis on $b$. \end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Sign square]\label{lem:sgnsq}
$\sgn(b)\cdot\sgn(b)=1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof} Both $(+1)^2$ and $(-1)^2$ equal $1$. \end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Cardinality]\label{lem:card}
$\lvert\cube\rvert=2^n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof} The number of functions $\{1,\dots,n\}\to\{\mathrm{true},\mathrm{false}\}$
is $2^n$. \end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Involution]\label{lem:inv}
$\flip_i\circ\flip_i=\mathrm{id}$; hence $\flip_i$ is a bijection of $\cube$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof} Toggling bit $i$ twice is the identity; other coordinates are
untouched. \end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Flip negates own coordinate]\label{lem:flipself}
$\coord(\flip_i(x),i)=-\coord(x,i)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof} The $i$-th bit becomes $\lnot x_i$; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:sgnnot}. \end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Flip fixes other coordinates]\label{lem:flipne}
If $j\neq i$ then $\coord(\flip_i(x),j)=\coord(x,j)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof} $\flip_i$ does not change the $j$-th bit. \end{proof}

Because $\flip_i$ is a permutation of the summation index set, for any $g$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:reindex}
\sum_{x} g(x)=\sum_{x} g(\flip_i(x)).
\end{equation}

\section{Main results}

\subsection{Centering}

\begin{theorem}[Coordinates are centered]\label{thm:centered}
For every index $i$, $\displaystyle\sum_{x\in\cube}\coord(x,i)=0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply \eqref{eq:reindex} with the flip on coordinate $i$ and
$g(x)=\coord(x,i)$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:flipself},
\[
\sum_x \coord(x,i)=\sum_x \coord(\flip_i(x),i)=\sum_x\big(-\coord(x,i)\big)=-\sum_x\coord(x,i).
\]
A real number equal to its own negative is zero.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Functionals are centered]\label{cor:Einner}
Every linear functional is centered: $\E[\inner{\theta}{x}]=0$ for all $\theta$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
By linearity, $\E[\sum_k\theta_k\coord(\cdot,k)]=\sum_k\theta_k\E[\coord(\cdot,k)]=0$
by Theorem~\ref{thm:centered}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Identity covariance}

\begin{theorem}[Covariance kernel is the identity]\label{thm:covariance}
For all $k,l$,
\[
T(k,l)=\begin{cases}2^n & k=l,\\ 0 & k\neq l.\end{cases}
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
\emph{Off-diagonal.} Suppose $k\neq l$. Apply \eqref{eq:reindex} with the flip on
coordinate $k$ and $g(x)=\coord(x,k)\coord(x,l)$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:flipself} the
$k$-factor negates and by Lemma~\ref{lem:flipne} the $l$-factor is unchanged (since
$l\neq k$), so $g(\flip_k(x))=-g(x)$ and $T(k,l)=-T(k,l)=0$.

\emph{Diagonal.} By Lemma~\ref{lem:sgnsq} the summand $\coord(x,k)^2=1$ for every
$x$; summing the constant $1$ over the $2^n$ points (Lemma~\ref{lem:card}) gives
$T(k,k)=2^n$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Isotropy: a Pythagorean second-moment identity}

\begin{theorem}[Second-moment identity]\label{thm:isotropy}
For every coefficient vector $\theta=(\theta_1,\dots,\theta_n)$,
\[
\sum_{x\in\cube}\Big(\sum_k\theta_k\,\coord(x,k)\Big)^2=2^n\sum_k\theta_k^2,
\qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad
\E\big[\inner{\theta}{x}^2\big]=\sum_k\theta_k^2=\norm{\theta}^2.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expand the square pointwise,
\[
\Big(\sum_k\theta_k\coord(x,k)\Big)^2=\sum_k\sum_l\theta_k\theta_l\,\coord(x,k)\coord(x,l).
\]
Sum over $x$ and exchange the order of summation so the $x$-sum is innermost; it
equals $T(k,l)$:
\[
\sum_x\Big(\sum_k\theta_k\coord(x,k)\Big)^2=\sum_k\sum_l\theta_k\theta_l\,T(k,l).
\]
Substitute Theorem~\ref{thm:covariance}: off-diagonal terms vanish and each
diagonal term contributes $\theta_k^2\cdot 2^n$, collapsing the double sum to
$2^n\sum_k\theta_k^2$. Dividing by $2^n$ gives the expectation form.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
This is a Parseval/Pythagorean identity: the coordinate functions
$\coord(\cdot,k)$ form an orthonormal system for the uniform inner product
$\langle f,g\rangle=\E[fg]$, and the squared norm of a linear combination is the
sum of squared coefficients---the $n$-dimensional Pythagorean theorem.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Dimension-free isotropy}

\begin{theorem}[Dimension-free isotropy]\label{thm:dimfree}
For every unit vector $\theta$ (i.e.\ $\sum_k\theta_k^2=1$),
\[
\E\big[\inner{\theta}{x}^2\big]=1,
\]
independently of the dimension $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:isotropy} with $\norm{\theta}^2=1$.
\end{proof}

The covariance matrix of the uniform measure on $\cube$ is the identity $I_n$ in
every dimension. Hence the discrete cube is exactly in isotropic position with
isotropic constant $L=1$, uniformly in $n$: every unit functional sees variance
$1$, and there is no thin direction. This is the discrete realization of the
structural premise of the slicing conjecture---a bounded, indeed constant,
dimension-free isotropic constant.

\section{Algorithmic content}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Exact covariance kernel by full enumeration.} Enumerate all $2^n$
corners, accumulate outer products $\coord(x,\cdot)\coord(x,\cdot)^\top$, and
confirm the result equals $2^n I_n$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:covariance}). Time
$\Theta(2^n n^2)$, space $\Theta(n^2)$; feasible for $n\lesssim 20$.
\item \textbf{Directional second-moment estimator.} For a fixed unit $\theta$,
compute $\E[\inner{\theta}{x}^2]$ exactly ($\Theta(2^n n)$) or by Monte Carlo
($\Theta(Sn)$ for $S$ samples) and compare to the predicted value $1$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:dimfree}). The estimator concentrates around $1$ independently of
$n$.
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion}

The dimension-fragile heart of the slicing problem is the uniform control of the
isotropic constant; all routes pass through isotropic position and the covariance
matrix. The discrete cube isolates exactly this mechanism---identity covariance
$\Rightarrow$ dimension-free isotropy---while removing the measure-theoretic
apparatus that obscures it. The proofs of Theorems~\ref{thm:centered} and
\ref{thm:covariance} are the \emph{same} one-line argument applied to different
summands: a sign-reversing involution forces a sum to equal its negative, hence
zero. This robustness is precisely what makes the conclusion dimension-free: the
symmetry exists in every dimension and does not weaken. Slicing additionally
requires anti-concentration of marginals $\inner{\theta}{x}$, which for the
discrete cube is the classical Littlewood--Offord--Erd\H{o}s phenomenon.

\section{Future work}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Tensorization.} The covariance of a product measure is
block-diagonal, so products of isotropic models remain isotropic; the unit second
moment splits as $\E_\mu[\inner{\theta}{\cdot}^2]=\E_{\mu_1}[\inner{\theta_1}{\cdot}^2]
+\E_{\mu_2}[\inner{\theta_2}{\cdot}^2]$ with $\lvert\theta_1\rvert^2+\lvert\theta_2\rvert^2=1$.
\item \textbf{Affine invariance.} A box (weighted cube with nonzero weights $a_k$)
has, after volume normalization by $(\prod_k a_k^2)^{-1/n}$, determinant-one
covariance and the same normalized second-moment functional as the unit cube.
\item \textbf{Lower bound.} For products of symmetric two-point measures,
conjecture $\min_{\lvert\theta\rvert=1}\E[\inner{\theta}{x}^2]\geq c(\det\Sigma)^{1/n}$
with universal $c>0$, provable in the $c=1$ case via AM--GM on the eigenvalues
$a_k^2$.
\item \textbf{Anti-concentration.} Formalize $\max_t\Pr[\inner{\theta}{x}=t]\leq
C/\sqrt{n}$ for spread $\theta$ (Littlewood--Offord / Erd\H{o}s).
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

On the corners of a cube, the structural miracle behind Bourgain's slicing problem
becomes visible and fully verifiable: a single sign-flip involution forces the
covariance matrix of the uniform measure on $\cube$ to be the identity in every
dimension, so every unit functional has variance exactly $1$, dimension-free. The
resulting identity $\E[\inner{\theta}{x}^2]=\norm{\theta}^2$ is
Pythagorean---orthonormal coordinates, sum of squares---and exhibits the discrete
cube as an exactly isotropic body with isotropic constant $1$.

